 # Progressive and Multi-stage Training
-
-
 ## Overview
-Progressive and Multi-stage Training refers to a family of optimization strategies that decompose the training of 3D Gaussian Splatting (3DGS) representations into sequential phases [G]. This approach addresses convergence instability and error propagation inherent in optimizing all parameters simultaneously, particularly when dealing with complex geometry, sparse inputs, or feed-forward pose estimation [15018-multi-stage-training-with-pose-prediction.md]. By decoupling geometry initialization, appearance refinement, and high-frequency detail capture, these methods ensure that the Gaussian distribution adapts progressively to the scene's complexity [7173-hybrid-coarse-to-fine-strategy.md]. Unlike single-stage approaches, multi-stage methods isolate specific learning objectives (e.g., pose vs. attributes) to stabilize the optimization landscape [15018-multi-stage-training-with-pose-prediction.md].
+Progressive and Multi-stage Training refers to a family of optimization strategies that decompose the training of 3D Gaussian Splatting (3DGS) representations into sequential phases [G]. This approach addresses convergence instability and error propagation inherent in optimizing all parameters simultaneously, particularly when dealing with complex geometry, sparse inputs, or feed-forward pose estimation [G]. By decoupling geometry initialization, appearance refinement, and high-frequency detail capture, these methods ensure that the Gaussian distribution adapts progressively to the scene's complexity [G]. Unlike single-stage approaches, multi-stage methods isolate specific learning objectives (e.g., pose vs. attributes) to stabilize the optimization landscape [G]. It sets itself apart from sibling methods by explicitly partitioning the optimization timeline into sequential phases with distinct trainable parameter sets and supervision signals, rather than applying a single unified training loop.
 
